load @/code/structure/manifold
  find manifold

# Curvature, the local bending of a space, and the constant negative curvature that defines hyperbolic geometry. The
# sign of the curvature is read off triangles: fat (angle sum above two right angles) is positive, exact is flat,
# thin (angle sum below) is negative.

# Gaussian curvature: the single number measuring how a surface bends at a point, the product of its two principal
# curvatures, intrinsic (it can be felt without leaving the surface, by measuring triangles and circles). The
# hyperbolic plane has constant NEGATIVE Gaussian curvature everywhere. The form carries the manifold.
form gaussian-curvature
  link manifold, like manifold

# Constant curvature: the curvature is the same at every point and in every direction, so the space is homogeneous (it
# looks the same from everywhere) and isotropic (the same in every direction). There are exactly three such geometries:
# the sphere (positive), the flat plane (zero), and the hyperbolic plane (negative). The substrate's bulk is the
# negative one.
form constant-curvature
  link manifold, like manifold

# The Gauss-Bonnet theorem: it ties the total curvature of a region to its boundary turning and its topology, and for a
# geodesic triangle it says the area times the curvature equals the angle DEFECT (two right angles minus the angle sum).
# In hyperbolic space, with negative curvature, the defect is positive and equals the area, so area is bounded by the
# ideal triangle's. The form carries the manifold.
form gauss-bonnet
  link manifold, like manifold

# Sectional curvature: in dimension three and up, the curvature of each two-dimensional slice through a point. A
# hyperbolic space has all sectional curvatures equal and negative, which is the higher-dimensional meaning of
# "constant negative curvature" for the dodecagrid and the 120-cell grid. The form carries the manifold.
form sectional-curvature
  link manifold, like manifold
